 and  documentaries  which are not likely to be commercially  viable and that's i think it's really  important to maintain that competition  and i do think channel 4 is part of that  ecosystem  he also told me his panel that he would  never he didn't even contemplate selling  um channel 4 when he was culture  secretary your thoughts on the  suggestions from nadine doris this  morning that that's the way forward  well i mean i think i agree with with  jeremy hunt there i just don't see  what the case is  uh for selling off uh channel four i  mean not notwithstanding that it's just  not anybody's priority right now there's  plenty of other things that the  government should be getting on with  like dealing with the cost of living um  crisis and so on but channel four is a  non-for-profit publicly owned uh  broadcasting uh company whose remit is  very specific and and special and unique  in the uk in that it's there to support  small independent production companies  it doesn't own the ip itself it doesn't  own those programs itself and so that  nurtures and allows lots of small  businesses to develop particularly  outside of london and channel 4 do a  great deal in terms of investing in that  leveling up agenda as well so i just  don't understand why they're doing it  nobody's in favor of it the the industry  is not in favor of channel 4 itself  isn't in favor i the public are quite  happy with things uh as they are so it  really does feel like a the wrong  decision at the wrong time and you know  it feels like it is a cultural vandalism  um a bit of a vendetta if you like  against some of the news output that the  government just doesn't like  um talk to me about the cancer care  report um that we're going to be hearing  more about today the way you're speaking  to us from this morning the northwest  liverpool particularly  has got the most challenges when it  comes  to cancer cases in the whole  of england  it looks as though potentially we're  moving backwards when it comes to  treating cancer what do you  what does labour put that down to  well look i mean  we have we we've historically have  always had some of the  the worst cancer treatment um uh rates  we rece in recent times we've reversed  that trend and we've seen and treated  people much more quickly obviously the  last labor government brought in very  fast targets for cancer treatment and  diagnosis which absolutely uh sped up  the the system and dealt with with some  of those um those deep inequalities in  in terms of care that we we had  historically uh been seeing there's no  question that the uh the pandemic and  what we saw in terms of the nhs has put  huge set that backwards really there's  no question that people  didn't go to hospital perhaps didn't go  to see their doctor in in early stages  when they had worries they had concerns  and and that's going to have a have a  lag as well and obviously the nhs is is  dealing with a huge uh backlog here and  it hasn't got the staff it hasn't got  the resources that it needs to to really  deal with uh to deal with those all-time  high waiting list that it's now got and  that's obviously going to have a  dramatic effect on on things like cancer  care and other areas as well  i don't know if you heard um the whale  secretary was on the program uh  yesterday  saying the world's moved on and as far  as partygate is concerned the prime  minister will be staying in post as he  should  i would clip that up on twitter more  than half a million people viewed that  on my twitter feed yesterday as you can  imagine it was a a myriad of different  comments what's your view  well  i mean people won't forget partygate  that easily i'm afraid i mean the  government might want to brush it under  the carpet  um but it really matters it really  matters because of the sacrifices that  everybody else made in not  seeing their loved ones in not attending  funerals and not seeing people who were  dying in in hospital or or being able to  